0951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129320 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1045 "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129340 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bf654963-a5d1-5562-85bc-b7c2fbbe6d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "497a1af7-923f-5a2e-82d5-147a5d2a1d6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129488 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130300 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130300 #edb/ref 1061 618749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130300 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5c27e9e-03cb-5a93-8a56-bd33588960fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130300 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130301 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130301 #edb/ref 1061 618750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130301 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "031d5fb3-1834-595b-8a36-577430a0f9a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130301 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130302 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130302 #edb/ref 1061 618751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130302 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6a3e1f3-c536-58d3-a81e-7966262884ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130302 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1061 618754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "622390da-a9e6-55af-8449-1ceed5d4cec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130303 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1061 618755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddd7ced1-9fe0-533d-bd7a-9dfbcff663f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1061 618758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f4e64a8-3397-5e7e-b805-080c59c56440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1065 6113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1064 "When we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi x \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in the case of a parametrically defined curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1065 6145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "548401c3-a52c-5444-b834-57c50c4bfa72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130450 #edb/ref 1067 "Surface Area Generated by Functions of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1065 7707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130466 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428530 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428530 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1995->3770. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428530 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048245 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186530549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186530550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186530555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186530558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186530563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1045 "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186530558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529573 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f6065dfd-4199-50b0-9334-da4b9f494b0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529665 #edb/ref 1080 "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529665 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529665 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "276f10df-f807-54a9-b01f-7cf1b357ec66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529841 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529872 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}